\caption(The shaded red faces and blue fan come from actual trace entries and the final core output. The square's circumcircle is derived from its prescribed coordinates. Its dashed outline passes through all four corners; the red centre is strictly inside.)
\alttext(Two side-by-side square panels share equal coordinate scales. The left has a diagonal splitting two red triangles inside a dashed circle, while the right has four blue triangles meeting at the red centre site four.)

> Circle signs, cavity replacement and the symbolic boundary

For counterclockwise a,b,c, the orientation-sensitive determinant

\[
I(a,b,c,d)=\det\begin{pmatrix}
a_x&a_y&a_x^2+a_y^2&1\\
b_x&b_y&b_x^2+b_y^2&1\\
c_x&c_y&c_x^2+c_y^2&1\\
d_x&d_y&d_x^2+d_y^2&1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
\alttext(The incircle determinant has one row per point, with columns x, y, squared distance from the origin, and one.)

is positive for d strictly inside the circumcircle, negative outside and
zero on it. Reversing a,b,c reverses the sign. The circle interpretation
requires a noncollinear first triple. Lifting each point to z=x²+y² turns
the circle test into a side-of-plane test; subtracting the circle's linear
plane from the lift gives the sign relation. Computing a centre and radius
by division is unnecessary for this topological decision.

Bowyer–Watson insertion removes every conflicting finite triangle and
replaces its cavity by triangles joining the new site to the nonshared
boundary edges. Edges occurring twice are internal to the cavity and
cancel; edges occurring once bound it. The geometric cavity argument
preserves a planar Delaunay triangulation when the initial boundary and
sign decisions are valid. No strictly interior point is allowed, while
points exactly on a circumcircle are permitted. The implementation retains
existing finite faces on exact cocircular ties, so the same input order
is deterministic but another order may select another valid diagonal.

A finite enclosing triangle is an unreliable initialization: containing
all *sites* does not ensure that its vertices lie outside all circles that
matter. Narrow triples can have arbitrarily large circumcircles relative
to their bounding box. The former twenty-extent super-triangle returned
no face for the three noncollinear sites (0,0), (1,0), (0.5,0.000001).
This failure was reproduced before replacing the code; the native example
below now returns its one required face.

The replacement begins with a real noncollinear seed and three symbolic
faces outside its hull edges. A ghost stores a reversed finite hull edge
and an infinity marker, with no fabricated coordinates. Its conflict test
is the exact exterior half-plane test; a point exactly between the edge's
endpoints conflicts too, allowing boundary-edge insertion without a zero-
area face. Cavity replacement handles finite and ghost faces uniformly,
rotating a new ghost so its marker stays last. Final output discards ghosts.
The analogous symbolic-boundary design already present in the repository's
CAD triangulator motivates this initialization; CAD implementation and
public geometry API remain separate callers.

Finite validation precedes even empty results or resource caps. Equal
coordinates, including signed zeros, coalesce to the earliest original id;
output references those original indices. All-collinear or fewer than three
distinct sites have no two-dimensional triangulation. Final finite faces
are strictly CCW, rotated to their smallest id and sorted. These ordering
and degeneracy rules are explicit rather than consequences of hash traversal.

> Retain the original seeded cloud and check its counts

The native cloud generator keeps the original mt19937 seed 20250728, thirty
records, square extent ten, and x-then-y uniform draws. The distribution
implementation can vary across C++ libraries, so this seed identifies a
reproducible control within the same toolchain rather than universal point
bytes. The core generator replaces report-local sampling code.

For u distinct noncollinear sites and h sites on the convex-hull boundary,
including sites collinear along hull edges, a complete planar triangulation
satisfies

\[
u-E+F=1,\qquad 3F=2E-h,\qquad
F=2u-2-h,\quad E=3u-3-h.
\]
\alttext(Euler's disk relation and edge incidence give two u minus two minus h finite faces, and three u minus three minus h edges.)

The first identity omits the exterior face. Every boundary edge is counted
once among triangle sides, every interior edge twice. These relations
assume coverage, nonoverlap and use of all distinct sites; matching a count
alone does not establish those assumptions. The native analysis also counts
used sites and checks every nonvertex distinct site against every supplied
face's exact circle. It validates indices, CCW orientation, duplicate faces
and edge multiplicity, but does not certify the global embedding of an
arbitrary supplied mesh. Independent tests cover that additional geometry.
The figure uses equal geometric scales; floating minimum angle is only a
summary, never an input to a topological decision.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/delaunay.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto p=dm::geometry::delaunay_uniform_cloud(30);const auto t=dm::geometry::delaunay_trace(p);const auto a=dm::geometry::delaunay_analyze(p,t.triangles);
  const auto predicted=2*a.distinct_points-2-a.boundary_edges;
  if(a.distinct_points!=30 || a.used_points!=30 || t.triangles.size()!=predicted || a.interior_violations)return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(85,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke edge;edge.color={96,165,250};edge.width=1.2;
  for(const auto f:t.triangles)d.polygon({{p[f.a].x,p[f.a].y},{p[f.b].x,p[f.b].y},{p[f.c].x,p[f.c].y}},{239,246,255},edge);
  for(const auto& q:p)d.ellipse({q.x,q.y},.045,.045,{220,38,38});
  d.text({5,10.8},"Original seeded 30-site cloud: complete finite Delaunay mesh",23)
   .text({5,-.8},std::format("u={}; h={}; F={}; E={}; exact interior-circle violations={}",a.distinct_points,a.boundary_edges,a.triangles,a.edges,a.interior_violations),21)
   .save_svg("build/delaunay.svg");
  std::print("| records | distinct sites | used sites | boundary h | edges E | faces F | predicted F | exact circle queries | interior violations | min angle (degrees) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} |\n",p.size(),a.distinct_points,a.used_points,a.boundary_edges,a.edges,a.triangles,predicted,a.circle_tests,a.interior_violations,a.minimum_angle_degrees);
  std::print("\nActual solver conflict scans: {} finite incircle tests + {} ghost half-plane tests = {}.\n",t.work.incircle_tests,t.work.hull_side_tests,t.work.incircle_tests+t.work.hull_side_tests);
}
