// result_end
\caption(The original random-control inputs are generated in the canonical C++ library. Every returned finite face is displayed, and every nonvertex distinct site is tested against every face's circumcircle. Minimum angle is measured rather than inferred from the Delaunay name.)
\alttext(A blue triangulation covers thirty red points in a square region. A computed footer reports the number of distinct sites, hull-boundary edges, finite faces and edges, with zero exact circle violations.)

The reproduced cloud has 47 finite faces and eleven boundary sites, matching
the count formula. Its measured smallest angle is only 0.364812 degrees.
Thus even the original random control contains a very thin triangle despite
all 1,269 exact circle queries reporting no interior violation.

> Exact topology still permits arbitrarily thin triangles

A Delaunay triangulation maximizes the smallest angle among triangulations
of a fixed planar site set, with ties possible. This does not supply a
positive minimum-angle guarantee independent of the input. With only the
three sites (0,0), (1,0), (1/2,ε), there is exactly one possible triangle.
For 0<ε≤1/4 its smallest angle is atan(2ε), tending to zero with ε. Its
circumcircle is empty of other sites because there are none. The resulting
thin triangle is therefore Delaunay and unavoidable without modifying the
sites or adding refinement points. This corrects the original claim that
empty circumcircles explain the absence of thin triangles.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/delaunay_angles.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> log_height,log_angle,log_theory;
  std::print("| apex height epsilon | finite faces | minimum angle (degrees) | atan(2 epsilon), degrees |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(double h:{.25,.125,.0625,.01,.001,.0001,.00001,.000001}) {
    const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> p{{0,0},{1,0},{.5,h}};const auto f=dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation(p);const auto a=dm::geometry::delaunay_analyze(p,f);
    const double theory=std::atan(2*h)*180/std::acos(-1.);
    if(f.size()!=1 || a.interior_violations || std::abs(a.minimum_angle_degrees-theory)>1e-10)return 1;
    log_height.push_back(std::log10(h));log_angle.push_back(std::log10(a.minimum_angle_degrees));log_theory.push_back(std::log10(theory));
    std::print("| {:.6g} | {} | {:.9g} | {:.9g} |\n",h,f.size(),a.minimum_angle_degrees,theory);
  }
  const double below=std::nextafter(1.,0.),above=std::nextafter(1.,2.),tiny=std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min(),big=std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
  const int on=dm::geometry::incircle_sign({0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{1,1}),inside=dm::geometry::incircle_sign({0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{1,below}),outside=dm::geometry::incircle_sign({0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{1,above});
  const int small=dm::geometry::incircle_sign({-tiny,0},{0,-tiny},{tiny,0},{0,0}),large=dm::geometry::incircle_sign({-big,0},{0,-big},{big,0},{0,0});
  if(on!=0 || inside!=1 || outside!=-1 || small!=1 || large!=1)return 1;
  std::print("\nExact binary64 circle signs: on {}, one-ULP inward {}, one-ULP outward {}; smallest-subnormal circle centre {}, largest-finite circle centre {}.\n",on,inside,outside,small,large);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(log_height,log_angle,"native angle",{37,99,235},2).points(log_height,log_theory,"atan prediction",{31,41,55},4)
   .legend({"native angle","atan prediction"},{{37,99,235},{31,41,55}})
   .title("An empty circumcircle does not bound the smallest angle away from zero")
   .x_label("log10(apex height epsilon)").y_label("log10(minimum angle in degrees)")
   .x_limits(-6.3,-.3).y_limits(-4.3,1.8).size(1100,580).save_svg("build/delaunay_angles.svg");
}
