> Verification and scope

Nine new native tests pass AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
All 512 subsets of a 3×3 integer grid, in forward and reverse input order,
and 250 seeded integer point sets are checked with independent signed-128-bit
orientation/incircle arithmetic. Their checks include every used site,
noncrossing edges, exact hull/face area equality, boundary sites including
collinear hull points, Euler counts, CCW faces and every nonvertex circle
query. Separate actual-event replay checks removed faces, directed boundary
cancellation, replacements, live counts and accumulated work. All square
permutations, duplicate/signed-zero identities, collinear and edge-insertion
cases, ownership, invalid meshes, complete caps and the narrow regression
pass. Mixed largest-finite/subnormal cases exercise topology without floating
coordinate products. The predicate also agrees with an independent Python
Fraction translated-determinant oracle on 6,000 random full-range binary64
quadruples and 4,000 cocircular or one-ULP-perturbed controls. That optional
development oracle is separate from report execution; every report input,
solver, measurement and figure is native C++.

Existing Delaunay, Voronoi, jump-and-walk, finite-element and geometry-format
suites check consumers of the same public API. These checks distinguish
an exact local circle property from proof of an arbitrary mesh's global
embedding. The solver supplies an unconstrained triangulation of the sites'
convex hull, with deterministic degeneracy rules and complete results.
Uniform square clouds demonstrate its behavior; they do not establish
universal mesh quality or justify an external dataset for this geometric
study. Any fixed positive minimum-angle target requires additional input
assumptions or refinement beyond this API.

> References

- Adrian Bowyer (1981), *Computing Dirichlet tessellations*, The Computer Journal 24(2), 162–166. [Original paper DOI](https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162).
- David F. Watson (1981), *Computing the n-dimensional Delaunay tessellation with application to Voronoi polytopes*, The Computer Journal 24(2), 167–172. [Original paper DOI](https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167). This core uses the cavity-insertion idea with symbolic boundary faces, exact decoded-dyadic signs and earliest duplicate identities.
- Jonathan R. Shewchuk (1997), *Adaptive Precision Floating-Point Arithmetic and Fast Robust Geometric Predicates*, Discrete & Computational Geometry 18, 305–363. [Author's paper and predicate resources](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~quake/robust.html). The determinant-sign motivation applies here; the fixed-integer implementation is distinct from the adaptive-expansion method.
- Jonathan R. Shewchuk, [Triangle quality meshing](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~quake/triangle.quality.html) and [Delaunay triangulations](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~quake/triangle.delaunay.html). Unconstrained triangulation, constrained boundaries and quality refinement are separate capabilities.
